From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: show GL frames on the display's refresh, at the guest's rate

The guest's vblank timer runs at the Mac display's rate (the EDID carries
it) but at its own phase. Shown as soon as the GPU finished them, two
guest frames sometimes landed in one refresh of the Mac and none in the
next: at a steady 120 frames a second from the guest only 107-109 distinct
frames a second reached the 120 Hz MacBook panel (ScreenCaptureKit count,
bench lock), 15-20 % of frames were never seen and 5-10 % of refreshes
repeated one. The guest looked smooth from the inside (Chrome's rAF:
120.01 fps, p99 8.4 ms) and juddered on the Mac.

Finished frames now wait in a short queue (a jitter buffer, at most three)
and once per tick, 3 ms before the vsync, the oldest one is put on the
layer. A display link on the window's screen (on a thread of its own: the
main thread ran ticks milliseconds late now and then) times it and follows
the window to other screens (60, 120, 144 Hz). While the guest draws
clearly faster than the link, a frame that comes less than half a tick
after the previous one replaces it, and QEMU blits at most one frame per
refresh of the window's screen (the newest), as when frames are shown
when drawn, not one per flush; at the link's own rate two close frames
are jitter, both are shown at once. Once a second, if a frame was left over
after every tick and none came late, one frame is skipped, so the queue
never holds more delay than the guest's timing needs (the guest's EDID
rate is a hair faster than the display's: 120.006 vs 120.000 Hz). Five
present surfaces instead of three: one on screen, one committed but not
latched yet, up to three queued (three stay with OMACVM_GL_VSYNC=0).

The refresh rate follows the guest, like a native app on ProMotion. The
link runs only while frames come close together (under 52 ms apart:
animations, scrolling, video); frames that come one at a time (typing, a
cursor, a clock) go on the layer as soon as they are ready and nothing
ticks. An idle Omarchy desktop sends no frames at all. On a
variable-refresh screen (NSScreen's longest refresh interval above its
shortest: the MacBook panel, 24-120 Hz) the link asks for the slowest
whole fraction of the full rate that is a whole multiple of the guest's
frame rate, measured over a second: 24 frames a second get 24 Hz, 30 get
30, 60 get 60, 20 get 40; 25, irregular frames and 120 get the full rate.
A lower rate needs two seconds in a row; two extra frames in a row (or a
full queue) bring the full rate back at once. Screens with one rate (60 Hz
externals, virtual displays) keep it.

Results, final build (bench lock, virtual 120 Hz display, 3 x 12 s):
testufo.com 119.6-119.9 new frames a second, 1-5 skipped refreshes in
12 s; gpu-native's present 92.0-100.5 with 220-251 (measured beside an
earlier build of this patch). With the MacBook's 24 Hz floor faked (dev
build) 116.2-119.2 with 3-4 skips, and the link stayed at 120 Hz with no
rate change. Round 1 on the MacBook panel: 99.0-99.6 % of a 120 fps
page's frames shown exactly once (74-82 %), 119.3-119.9 in testufo full
screen; external 60 Hz 99.7 % (82 %). Flush to screen about one refresh
more than showing frames when drawn (12.6-14.2 ms median vs 5-7 ms).
Frames of a fixed-rate page held on screen exactly as long as they should,
with the rate following the guest (24 Hz floor): 24 fps 97.9 % (link at
24 Hz), 30 fps 97.7 %, 60 fps 99.8 %; at the full rate 55.8 %, 98.3 %,
99.0 %. The link ticks 24, 30 or 60 times a second instead of 120, not at
all for a page that changes 5 times a second; QEMU's CPU 0-4 points lower.
glmark2 quick, 3 runs: median 2518 (2488 with OMACVM_GL_VSYNC=0).
30-minute soak (dev build with the 24 Hz floor, one VM run, 20 cycles of
pacing page, testufo, glmark2 and mpv): guest up throughout, no QEMU
errors, testufo 119.1-120.0 in 18 of 20 cycles (105.9 and 115.2 while
other VMs loaded the Mac and the guest itself drew 111-114 a second).

OMACVM_GL_VSYNC=0 goes back to showing frames when drawn.
OMACVM_GL_REFRESH=fixed keeps the link at the screen's full rate while
frames come (round 1's behaviour).
OMACVM_GL_LEAD_MS sets how long before the vsync a frame goes on the layer.
Trace events (-trace 'cocoa_present_*'): the link's rate, ticks, frames,
repeats, merged and dropped frames once a second, and each frame.

Upstream: not sent (depends on the IOSurface present, an OmacVM patch).
Tests: tests/graphics/pacing (pacing page + sckpace, testufo + ufopace,
pacing.html?fps=N + cadence.py for the rate, vdisplay for 144 Hz);
glmark2 quick for throughput. Applies after qemu-cocoa-gl-present-iosurface.patch.
